    {
      "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-41m",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "41M What this Part is about",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-41m"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf, p.154",
      "values:originalText": "This Part contains offences and civil penalty provisions that are\naimed at ensuring that:\n•\nthe essential principles are complied with (see Division 1);\n•\nthe conformity assessment procedures have been applied to\nkinds of medical devices or requirements, comparable to those\nprocedures, have been applied to kinds of medical devices\n(see Division 2);\n•\nadministrative processes put in place by Parts 4-4 to 4-9 are\nfollowed (see Divisions 3, 3A and 4).\nNote:\nThere are also some offences and civil penalty provisions in the earlier\nParts of this Chapter. They generally relate to matters ancillary to\nadministrative processes in those Parts (e.g. false or misleading\nstatements in applications).\n\nMedical devices  Chapter 4\nOffences and civil penalty provisions relating to medical devices  Part 4-11\nNon-compliance with essential principles  Division 1\nSection 41MA\nTherapeutic Goods Act 1989\nCompilation date: 05/09/2025",
      "skos:definition": "This Part contains offences and civil penalty provisions that are\naimed at ensuring that:\n•\nthe essential principles are complied with (see Division 1);\n•\nthe conformity assessment procedures have been applied to\nkinds of medical devices or requirements, comparable to those\nprocedures, have been applied to kinds of medical devices\n(see Division 2);\n•\nadministrative processes put in place by Parts 4-4 to 4-9 are\nfollowed (see Divisions 3, 3A and 4).\nNote:\nThere are also some offences and civil penalty provisions in the earlier\nParts of this Chapter. They generally relate to matters ancillary to\nadministrative processes in those Parts (e.g. false or misleading\nstatements in applications).\n\nMedical devices  Chapter 4\nOffences and civil penalty provisions relating to medical devices  Part 4-11\nNon-compliance with essential principles  Division 1\nSection 41MA\nTherapeutic Goods Act 1989\nCompilation date: 05/09/2025",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-41m-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-41m-logic-deontic-1"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "f1304f83d26611e913926e628e91b3f4a8606c67b2e46636875a8b971086cb34",
      "skos:note": "Ensure conformity with essential principles and administrative processes."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-41m",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "41M What this Part is about",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 154,
      "values:originalText": "This Part contains offences and civil penalty provisions that are\naimed at ensuring that:\n•\nthe essential principles are complied with (see Division 1);\n•\nthe conformity assessment procedures have been applied to\nkinds of medical devices or requirements, comparable to those\nprocedures, have been applied to kinds of medical devices\n(see Division 2);\n•\nadministrative processes put in place by Parts 4-4 to 4-9 are\nfollowed (see Divisions 3, 3A and 4).\nNote:\nThere are also some offences and civil penalty provisions in the earlier\nParts of this Chapter. They generally relate to matters ancillary to\nadministrative processes in those Parts (e.g. false or misleading\nstatements in applications).\n\nMedical devices  Chapter 4\nOffences and civil penalty provisions relating to medical devices  Part 4-11\nNon-compliance with essential principles  Division 1\nSection 41MA\nTherapeutic Goods Act 1989\nCompilation date: 05/09/2025"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-41m-norm",
      "@type": "values:Obligation",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-41m"
      },
      "values:deonticStatus": {
        "@id": "values:HeuristicDerived"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "values:borneBy": {
        "@id": "values:State"
      },
      "skos:scopeNote": "borne by: State"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-41m-logic-deontic-1",
      "@type": "cml:LogicApplication",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "cml:operator": "MUST",
      "cml:logicSummary": "Ensure compliance",
      "cml:executionSurface": "modalities::logic::deontic",
      "cml:confidence": {
        "@value": 1.0,
        "@type": "xsd:decimal"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:premiseText": "The essential principles must be complied with.",
      "cml:conclusionText": "There is an obligation to comply with the essential principles."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-41ma",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "41MA Criminal offences for importing, supplying or exporting a",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-41ma"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf, p.155",
      "values:originalText": "medical device that does not comply with essential\nprinciples\nOffences relating to importing a medical device\n(1) A person commits an offence if:\n(a) the person imports a medical device into Australia; and\n(b) the medical device does not comply with the essential\nprinciples relating to matters other than the labelling of the\ndevice; and\n(c) the Secretary has not consented to the importation; and\n(ca) the device is not of a kind covered by an exemption in force\nunder section 41GS; and\n(d) either:\n(i) the use of the device has resulted in, will result in, or is\nlikely to result in, harm or injury to any person; or\n(ii) the use of the device, if the device were used, would\nresult in, or would be likely to result in, harm or injury\nto any person; and\n(e) the harm or injury has resulted, will result, is likely to result,\nwould result, or would be likely to result, because the device\ndoes not comply with the essential principles.\nPenalty: Imprisonment for 5 years or 4,000 penalty units, or both.\nNote 1:\nA jury may acquit a person of an offence against this subsection and\nmay convict the person of an offence against subsection (4) instead:\nsee section 53A.\nNote 2:\nFor the liability of an executive officer of a body corporate, see\nsections 54B and 54BA.\n(4) A person commits an offence if:\n(a) the person imports a medical device into Australia; and\n\nChapter 4  Medical devices",
      "skos:definition": "medical device that does not comply with essential\nprinciples\nOffences relating to importing a medical device\n(1) A person commits an offence if:\n(a) the person imports a medical device into Australia; and\n(b) the medical device does not comply with the essential\nprinciples relating to matters other than the labelling of the\ndevice; and\n(c) the Secretary has not consented to the importation; and\n(ca) the device is not of a kind covered by an exemption in force\nunder section 41GS; and\n(d) either:\n(i) the use of the device has resulted in, will result in, or is\nlikely to result in, harm or injury to any person; or\n(ii) the use of the device, if the device were used, would\nresult in, or would be likely to result in, harm or injury\nto any person; and\n(e) the harm or injury has resulted, will result, is likely to result,\nwould result, or would be likely to result, because the device\ndoes not comply with the essential principles.\nPenalty: Imprisonment for 5 years or 4,000 penalty units, or both.\nNote 1:\nA jury may acquit a person of an offence against this subsection and\nmay convict the person of an offence against subsection (4) instead:\nsee section 53A.\nNote 2:\nFor the liability of an executive officer of a body corporate, see\nsections 54B and 54BA.\n(4) A person commits an offence if:\n(a) the person imports a medical device into Australia; and\n\nChapter 4  Medical devices",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-41ma-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-41ma-ss-4"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "74de24467bb2f3534934b35542942718c71a4add1bff7cd4f8c1e22f1afa0cc7"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-41ma",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "41MA Criminal offences for importing, supplying or exporting a",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 155,
      "values:originalText": "medical device that does not comply with essential\nprinciples\nOffences relating to importing a medical device\n(1) A person commits an offence if:\n(a) the person imports a medical device into Australia; and\n(b) the medical device does not comply with the essential\nprinciples relating to matters other than the labelling of the\ndevice; and\n(c) the Secretary has not consented to the importation; and\n(ca) the device is not of a kind covered by an exemption in force\nunder section 41GS; and\n(d) either:\n(i) the use of the device has resulted in, will result in, or is\nlikely to result in, harm or injury to any person; or\n(ii) the use of the device, if the device were used, would\nresult in, or would be likely to result in, harm or injury\nto any person; and\n(e) the harm or injury has resulted, will result, is likely to result,\nwould result, or would be likely to result, because the device\ndoes not comply with the essential principles.\nPenalty: Imprisonment for 5 years or 4,000 penalty units, or both.\nNote 1:\nA jury may acquit a person of an offence against this subsection and\nmay convict the person of an offence against subsection (4) instead:\nsee section 53A.\nNote 2:\nFor the liability of an executive officer of a body corporate, see\nsections 54B and 54BA.\n(4) A person commits an offence if:\n(a) the person imports a medical device into Australia; and\n\nChapter 4  Medical devices"
    },
