      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-41mc",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "41MC Criminal offences relating to breaching a condition of a",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-41mc"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf, p.161",
      "values:originalText": "consent\n(1) The consent of the Secretary under section 41MA or 41MAA may\nbe given:\n(a) unconditionally or subject to conditions; or\n(b) in respect of particular medical devices or kinds of medical\ndevices.\n(2) A person commits an offence if:\n(a) the person does an act or omits to do an act; and\n(b) the act or omission breaches a condition of a consent; and\n(c) the act or omission has resulted in, will result in, or is likely\nto result in, harm or injury to any person.\nPenalty: 2,000 penalty units.\nNote 1:\nA jury may acquit a person of an offence against this subsection and\nmay convict the person of an offence against subsection (5) instead:\nsee section 53A.\nNote 2:\nFor the liability of an executive officer of a body corporate, see\nsections 54B and 54BA.\n\nChapter 4  Medical devices",
      "skos:definition": "consent\n(1) The consent of the Secretary under section 41MA or 41MAA may\nbe given:\n(a) unconditionally or subject to conditions; or\n(b) in respect of particular medical devices or kinds of medical\ndevices.\n(2) A person commits an offence if:\n(a) the person does an act or omits to do an act; and\n(b) the act or omission breaches a condition of a consent; and\n(c) the act or omission has resulted in, will result in, or is likely\nto result in, harm or injury to any person.\nPenalty: 2,000 penalty units.\nNote 1:\nA jury may acquit a person of an offence against this subsection and\nmay convict the person of an offence against subsection (5) instead:\nsee section 53A.\nNote 2:\nFor the liability of an executive officer of a body corporate, see\nsections 54B and 54BA.\n\nChapter 4  Medical devices",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-41mc-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-41mc-ss-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "12423e0482f3e81cb1d23230664e95bb3d11b9f34f06fc199e588d4cdab6e4d4"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-41mc",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "41MC Criminal offences relating to breaching a condition of a",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 161,
      "values:originalText": "consent\n(1) The consent of the Secretary under section 41MA or 41MAA may\nbe given:\n(a) unconditionally or subject to conditions; or\n(b) in respect of particular medical devices or kinds of medical\ndevices.\n(2) A person commits an offence if:\n(a) the person does an act or omits to do an act; and\n(b) the act or omission breaches a condition of a consent; and\n(c) the act or omission has resulted in, will result in, or is likely\nto result in, harm or injury to any person.\nPenalty: 2,000 penalty units.\nNote 1:\nA jury may acquit a person of an offence against this subsection and\nmay convict the person of an offence against subsection (5) instead:\nsee section 53A.\nNote 2:\nFor the liability of an executive officer of a body corporate, see\nsections 54B and 54BA.\n\nChapter 4  Medical devices"
    },
