    {
      "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-41n",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "41N Simplified outline of this Chapter",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-41n"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf, p.200",
      "values:originalText": "The importation into Australia of vaping goods, and the\nmanufacture, supply or possession of vaping goods in Australia, is\nprohibited, subject to some exceptions. A person may commit an\noffence or be liable to a civil penalty for contravening the\nprohibitions (see Part 4A-2).\nDefinitions of vaping goods and related terms are set out in\nDivision 2 of this Part. Vaping goods includes vaping substances,\nvaping accessories, vaping devices and goods determined by the\nMinister to be vaping goods.\nThe Minister may, by legislative instrument, determine that\nspecified vaping goods, or a specified class of vaping goods, may\nbe supplied or possessed in Australia:\n(a)\nby a specified person, or a specified class of persons;\nand\n(b)\nin the circumstances (if any) specified in the\ndetermination; and\n(c)\nsubject to the conditions (if any) specified in the\ndetermination (see Part 4A-3).\nThe Secretary may, on application by a person, give the person\nconsent to manufacture, supply or possess vaping goods. The\nconsent may be given unconditionally or subject to conditions, and\nin respect of particular vaping goods or classes of vaping goods. In\ndeciding whether to give a consent, the Secretary must comply\nwith the decision-making principles (if any) determined by the\n\nVaping goods  Chapter 4A\nIntroduction  Part 4A-1\nIntroduction  Division 1\nSection 41NA\nTherapeutic Goods Act 1989\nCompilation date: 05/09/2025\nMinister by legislative instrument. A person may commit an\noffence or be liable to a civil penalty if conditions of a consent are\nbreached (see Part 4A-3).",
      "skos:definition": "The importation into Australia of vaping goods, and the\nmanufacture, supply or possession of vaping goods in Australia, is\nprohibited, subject to some exceptions. A person may commit an\noffence or be liable to a civil penalty for contravening the\nprohibitions (see Part 4A-2).\nDefinitions of vaping goods and related terms are set out in\nDivision 2 of this Part. Vaping goods includes vaping substances,\nvaping accessories, vaping devices and goods determined by the\nMinister to be vaping goods.\nThe Minister may, by legislative instrument, determine that\nspecified vaping goods, or a specified class of vaping goods, may\nbe supplied or possessed in Australia:\n(a)\nby a specified person, or a specified class of persons;\nand\n(b)\nin the circumstances (if any) specified in the\ndetermination; and\n(c)\nsubject to the conditions (if any) specified in the\ndetermination (see Part 4A-3).\nThe Secretary may, on application by a person, give the person\nconsent to manufacture, supply or possess vaping goods. The\nconsent may be given unconditionally or subject to conditions, and\nin respect of particular vaping goods or classes of vaping goods. In\ndeciding whether to give a consent, the Secretary must comply\nwith the decision-making principles (if any) determined by the\n\nVaping goods  Chapter 4A\nIntroduction  Part 4A-1\nIntroduction  Division 1\nSection 41NA\nTherapeutic Goods Act 1989\nCompilation date: 05/09/2025\nMinister by legislative instrument. A person may commit an\noffence or be liable to a civil penalty if conditions of a consent are\nbreached (see Part 4A-3).",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-41n-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-41n-logic-deontic-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-41n-logic-descriptionlogic-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "cad61469cf3cd9b830367dd73078c4b15f4faec68642cc8a9d741531c7580378",
      "skos:note": "Importation of vaping goods and manufacture, supply or possession are prohibited in Australia."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-41n",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "41N Simplified outline of this Chapter",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 200,
      "values:originalText": "The importation into Australia of vaping goods, and the\nmanufacture, supply or possession of vaping goods in Australia, is\nprohibited, subject to some exceptions. A person may commit an\noffence or be liable to a civil penalty for contravening the\nprohibitions (see Part 4A-2).\nDefinitions of vaping goods and related terms are set out in\nDivision 2 of this Part. Vaping goods includes vaping substances,\nvaping accessories, vaping devices and goods determined by the\nMinister to be vaping goods.\nThe Minister may, by legislative instrument, determine that\nspecified vaping goods, or a specified class of vaping goods, may\nbe supplied or possessed in Australia:\n(a)\nby a specified person, or a specified class of persons;\nand\n(b)\nin the circumstances (if any) specified in the\ndetermination; and\n(c)\nsubject to the conditions (if any) specified in the\ndetermination (see Part 4A-3).\nThe Secretary may, on application by a person, give the person\nconsent to manufacture, supply or possess vaping goods. The\nconsent may be given unconditionally or subject to conditions, and\nin respect of particular vaping goods or classes of vaping goods. In\ndeciding whether to give a consent, the Secretary must comply\nwith the decision-making principles (if any) determined by the\n\nVaping goods  Chapter 4A\nIntroduction  Part 4A-1\nIntroduction  Division 1\nSection 41NA\nTherapeutic Goods Act 1989\nCompilation date: 05/09/2025\nMinister by legislative instrument. A person may commit an\noffence or be liable to a civil penalty if conditions of a consent are\nbreached (see Part 4A-3)."
    },
