    {
      "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-41qb",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "41QB Offences and civil penalty provision—supplying vaping goods",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-41qb"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf, p.206",
      "values:originalText": "Offences\n(1) A person commits an offence if the person supplies vaping goods\nin Australia.\nPenalty: Imprisonment for 7 years or 5,000 penalty units, or both.\nNote:\nFor the liability of an executive officer of a body corporate, see\nsections 54B and 54BA.\n(2) A person commits an offence of strict liability if the person\nsupplies vaping goods in Australia.\nPenalty: 200 penalty units.\n\nVaping goods  Chapter 4A\nOffences and civil penalty provisions relating to vaping goods  Part 4A-2\nGeneral  Division 1\nSection 41QB\nTherapeutic Goods Act 1989\nCompilation date: 05/09/2025\nCivil penalty provision\n(3) A person contravenes this subsection if the person supplies vaping\ngoods in Australia.\nMaximum civil penalty:\n(a) for an individual—7,000 penalty units; and\n(b) for a body corporate—70,000 penalty units.\n(4) A person who contravenes subsection (3) commits a separate\ncontravention of that subsection in respect of each unit of vaping\ngoods supplied by the person in Australia.\nNote:\nFor unit of vaping goods, see subsection 3(1).\nExceptions—general\n(5) Subsections (1) to (4) do not apply if:\n(a) subsections (6), (7) and (8) apply in relation to the supply of\nthe vaping goods by the person; or\n(b) subsections (9), (10) and (11) apply in relation to the supply\nof the vaping goods by the person.\nNote:\nThe person bears an evidential burden in relation to the matters in\nsubsections (6), (7) and (8) or subsections (9), (10) and (11): see\nsubsection 13.3(3) of the Criminal Code and section 41QE of this Act.\nExceptions—wholesale supply chain\n(6) This subsection applies in relation to the supply of the vaping\ngoods by the person if:\n(a) the vaping goods are therapeutic goods that are entered on\nthe Register; or\n(b) both of the following apply:\n(i) the vaping goods are therapeutic goods that are exempt\ngoods under regulations made for the purposes of\nsubsection 18(1) or an exempt device under regulations\nmade for the purposes of subsection 41HA(1), and the\nsponsor has given the Secretary a notice in compliance\nwith the exemption;\n\nChapter 4A  Vaping goods",
      "skos:definition": "Offences\n(1) A person commits an offence if the person supplies vaping goods\nin Australia.\nPenalty: Imprisonment for 7 years or 5,000 penalty units, or both.\nNote:\nFor the liability of an executive officer of a body corporate, see\nsections 54B and 54BA.\n(2) A person commits an offence of strict liability if the person\nsupplies vaping goods in Australia.\nPenalty: 200 penalty units.\n\nVaping goods  Chapter 4A\nOffences and civil penalty provisions relating to vaping goods  Part 4A-2\nGeneral  Division 1\nSection 41QB\nTherapeutic Goods Act 1989\nCompilation date: 05/09/2025\nCivil penalty provision\n(3) A person contravenes this subsection if the person supplies vaping\ngoods in Australia.\nMaximum civil penalty:\n(a) for an individual—7,000 penalty units; and\n(b) for a body corporate—70,000 penalty units.\n(4) A person who contravenes subsection (3) commits a separate\ncontravention of that subsection in respect of each unit of vaping\ngoods supplied by the person in Australia.\nNote:\nFor unit of vaping goods, see subsection 3(1).\nExceptions—general\n(5) Subsections (1) to (4) do not apply if:\n(a) subsections (6), (7) and (8) apply in relation to the supply of\nthe vaping goods by the person; or\n(b) subsections (9), (10) and (11) apply in relation to the supply\nof the vaping goods by the person.\nNote:\nThe person bears an evidential burden in relation to the matters in\nsubsections (6), (7) and (8) or subsections (9), (10) and (11): see\nsubsection 13.3(3) of the Criminal Code and section 41QE of this Act.\nExceptions—wholesale supply chain\n(6) This subsection applies in relation to the supply of the vaping\ngoods by the person if:\n(a) the vaping goods are therapeutic goods that are entered on\nthe Register; or\n(b) both of the following apply:\n(i) the vaping goods are therapeutic goods that are exempt\ngoods under regulations made for the purposes of\nsubsection 18(1) or an exempt device under regulations\nmade for the purposes of subsection 41HA(1), and the\nsponsor has given the Secretary a ",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-41qb-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-41qb-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-41qb-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-41qb-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-41qb-ss-5"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-41qb-ss-6"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "87d390231948a0c49033f78db1046b1c0ea049452008ec1792ea0a263799dcc6"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-41qb",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "41QB Offences and civil penalty provision—supplying vaping goods",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 206,
      "values:originalText": "Offences\n(1) A person commits an offence if the person supplies vaping goods\nin Australia.\nPenalty: Imprisonment for 7 years or 5,000 penalty units, or both.\nNote:\nFor the liability of an executive officer of a body corporate, see\nsections 54B and 54BA.\n(2) A person commits an offence of strict liability if the person\nsupplies vaping goods in Australia.\nPenalty: 200 penalty units.\n\nVaping goods  Chapter 4A\nOffences and civil penalty provisions relating to vaping goods  Part 4A-2\nGeneral  Division 1\nSection 41QB\nTherapeutic Goods Act 1989\nCompilation date: 05/09/2025\nCivil penalty provision\n(3) A person contravenes this subsection if the person supplies vaping\ngoods in Australia.\nMaximum civil penalty:\n(a) for an individual—7,000 penalty units; and\n(b) for a body corporate—70,000 penalty units.\n(4) A person who contravenes subsection (3) commits a separate\ncontravention of that subsection in respect of each unit of vaping\ngoods supplied by the person in Australia.\nNote:\nFor unit of vaping goods, see subsection 3(1).\nExceptions—general\n(5) Subsections (1) to (4) do not apply if:\n(a) subsections (6), (7) and (8) apply in relation to the supply of\nthe vaping goods by the person; or\n(b) subsections (9), (10) and (11) apply in relation to the supply\nof the vaping goods by the person.\nNote:\nThe person bears an evidential burden in relation to the matters in\nsubsections (6), (7) and (8) or subsections (9), (10) and (11): see\nsubsection 13.3(3) of the Criminal Code and section 41QE of this Act.\nExceptions—wholesale supply chain\n(6) This subsection applies in relation to the supply of the vaping\ngoods by the person if:\n(a) the vaping goods are therapeutic goods that are entered on\nthe Register; or\n(b) both of the following apply:\n(i) the vaping goods are therapeutic goods that are exempt\ngoods under regulations made for the purposes of\nsubsection 18(1) or an exempt device under regulations\nmade for the purposes of subsection 41HA(1), and the\nsponsor has given the Secretary a notice in compliance\nwith the exemption;\n\nChapter 4A  Vaping goods"
    },
