      "skos:prefLabel": "42V(6) Recall of therapeutic goods because of actual or potential",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-42v-ss-6"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf, p.276",
      "values:originalText": "(6) A person commits an offence if:\n(a) the person fails to comply with a requirement under\nsubsection (1) in relation to a supply of therapeutic goods;\nand\n(b) either:\n(i) the use of the goods has resulted in, will result in, or is\nlikely to result in, harm or injury to any person; or\n(ii) the use of the goods, if the goods were used, would\nresult in, or would be likely to result in, harm or injury\nto any person; and\n(c) the harm or injury has resulted, will result, is likely to result,\nwould result, or would be likely to result, because the person\nfailed to comply with the requirement.\nPenalty: Imprisonment for 5 years or 4,000 penalty units, or both.\nNote 1:\nA jury may acquit a person of an offence against this subsection and\nmay convict the person of an offence against subsection (6C) instead:\nsee section 53A.\nNote 2:\nFor the liability of an executive officer of a body corporate, see\nsections 54B and 54BA.",
      "skos:definition": "(6) A person commits an offence if:\n(a) the person fails to comply with a requirement under\nsubsection (1) in relation to a supply of therapeutic goods;\nand\n(b) either:\n(i) the use of the goods has resulted in, will result in, or is\nlikely to result in, harm or injury to any person; or\n(ii) the use of the goods, if the goods were used, would\nresult in, or would be likely to result in, harm or injury\nto any person; and\n(c) the harm or injury has resulted, will result, is likely to result,\nwould result, or would be likely to result, because the person\nfailed to comply with the requirement.\nPenalty: Imprisonment for 5 years or 4,000 penalty units, or both.\nNote 1:\nA jury may acquit a person of an offence against this subsection and\nmay convict the person of an offence against subsection (6C) instead:\nsee section 53A.\nNote 2:\nFor the liability of an executive officer of a body corporate, see\nsections 54B and 54BA.",
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-42v"
      },
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-42v-ss-6-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-42v-ss-6-logic-deontic-1"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "07cc0436c56b92798e48f64d1b8b47032035611fec896ed88bd0cdee834ed52b",
      "skos:note": "Failure to comply with a requirement under subsection (1) in relation to a supply of therapeutic goods is an offence."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-42v-ss-6",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "42V(6) Recall of therapeutic goods because of actual or potential",
      "values:kind": "subsection",
      "cof:pageNumber": 276,
      "values:originalText": "(6) A person commits an offence if:\n(a) the person fails to comply with a requirement under\nsubsection (1) in relation to a supply of therapeutic goods;\nand\n(b) either:\n(i) the use of the goods has resulted in, will result in, or is\nlikely to result in, harm or injury to any person; or\n(ii) the use of the goods, if the goods were used, would\nresult in, or would be likely to result in, harm or injury\nto any person; and\n(c) the harm or injury has resulted, will result, is likely to result,\nwould result, or would be likely to result, because the person\nfailed to comply with the requirement.\nPenalty: Imprisonment for 5 years or 4,000 penalty units, or both.\nNote 1:\nA jury may acquit a person of an offence against this subsection and\nmay convict the person of an offence against subsection (6C) instead:\nsee section 53A.\nNote 2:\nFor the liability of an executive officer of a body corporate, see\nsections 54B and 54BA."
    },
