      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf, p.297",
      "values:originalText": "Restraining injunctions\n(1) The power of the Federal Court or Federal Circuit and Family\nCourt of Australia (Division 2) under this Part to grant an\ninjunction restraining a person from engaging in conduct may be\nexercised:\n(a) whether or not it appears to the court that the person intends\nto engage again, or to continue to engage, in conduct of that\nkind; and\n(b) whether or not the person has previously engaged in conduct\nof that kind; and\n(c) whether or not there is an imminent danger of substantial\ndamage to any other person if the person engages in conduct\nof that kind.\n\nChapter 5A  Enforcement",
      "skos:definition": "Restraining injunctions\n(1) The power of the Federal Court or Federal Circuit and Family\nCourt of Australia (Division 2) under this Part to grant an\ninjunction restraining a person from engaging in conduct may be\nexercised:\n(a) whether or not it appears to the court that the person intends\nto engage again, or to continue to engage, in conduct of that\nkind; and\n(b) whether or not the person has previously engaged in conduct\nof that kind; and\n(c) whether or not there is an imminent danger of substantial\ndamage to any other person if the person engages in conduct\nof that kind.\n\nChapter 5A  Enforcement",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-42yq-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-42yq-logic-deontic-1"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "acf5a9d3496a06038c2adcaa55b9d68e54f378d7a6a084e0481029a33178d9b3",
      "skos:note": "Court may grant injunction without considering intent or previous conduct."
    },
    {
