    {
      "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-46b",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "46B Searches and seizures on public health grounds",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-46b"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf, p.316",
      "values:originalText": "(1) Subject to subsection (2), if an authorised person has reasonable\ngrounds for suspecting that:\n(a) there may be on any premises a particular thing in respect of\nwhich this Act or the regulations have not been complied\nwith; and\n\nAdministration  Chapter 6\nEntry, searches and warrants  Part 6-2\n\nSection 47\nTherapeutic Goods Act 1989\nCompilation date: 05/09/2025\n(b) it is necessary in the interests of public health to exercise\npowers under this section in order to avoid an imminent risk\nof death, serious illness or serious injury;\nthe authorised person may, to the extent that it is reasonably\nnecessary for the purpose of avoiding an imminent risk of death,\nserious illness or serious injury, enter the premises and do any of\nthe following:\n(c) search the premises for the thing;\n(d) if the authorised person finds the thing on the premises—\nseize it.\n(2) An authorised person is not entitled to exercise any powers under\nsubsection (1) in relation to premises if:\n(a) the occupier of the premises has required the authorised\nperson to produce his or her identity card for inspection by\nthe occupier; and\n(b) the authorised person fails to comply with the requirement.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) Subject to subsection (2), if an authorised person has reasonable\ngrounds for suspecting that:\n(a) there may be on any premises a particular thing in respect of\nwhich this Act or the regulations have not been complied\nwith; and\n\nAdministration  Chapter 6\nEntry, searches and warrants  Part 6-2\n\nSection 47\nTherapeutic Goods Act 1989\nCompilation date: 05/09/2025\n(b) it is necessary in the interests of public health to exercise\npowers under this section in order to avoid an imminent risk\nof death, serious illness or serious injury;\nthe authorised person may, to the extent that it is reasonably\nnecessary for the purpose of avoiding an imminent risk of death,\nserious illness or serious injury, enter the premises and do any of\nthe following:\n(c) search the premises for the thing;\n(d) if the authorised person finds the thing on the premises—\nseize it.\n(2) An authorised person is not entitled to exercise any powers under\nsubsection (1) in relation to premises if:\n(a) the occupier of the premises has required the authorised\nperson to produce his or her identity card for inspection by\nthe occupier; and\n(b) the authorised person fails to comply with the requirement.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-46b-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-46b-ss-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "b91146a8538830085b2315f65d56ce828c83eba43783c6ee716ee3b92bae322c"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-46b",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "46B Searches and seizures on public health grounds",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 316,
      "values:originalText": "(1) Subject to subsection (2), if an authorised person has reasonable\ngrounds for suspecting that:\n(a) there may be on any premises a particular thing in respect of\nwhich this Act or the regulations have not been complied\nwith; and\n\nAdministration  Chapter 6\nEntry, searches and warrants  Part 6-2\n\nSection 47\nTherapeutic Goods Act 1989\nCompilation date: 05/09/2025\n(b) it is necessary in the interests of public health to exercise\npowers under this section in order to avoid an imminent risk\nof death, serious illness or serious injury;\nthe authorised person may, to the extent that it is reasonably\nnecessary for the purpose of avoiding an imminent risk of death,\nserious illness or serious injury, enter the premises and do any of\nthe following:\n(c) search the premises for the thing;\n(d) if the authorised person finds the thing on the premises—\nseize it.\n(2) An authorised person is not entitled to exercise any powers under\nsubsection (1) in relation to premises if:\n(a) the occupier of the premises has required the authorised\nperson to produce his or her identity card for inspection by\nthe occupier; and\n(b) the authorised person fails to comply with the requirement."
    },
