    {
      "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-48h",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "48H Retention of seized things",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-48h"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf, p.328",
      "values:originalText": "(1) Subject to any contrary order of a court, if an authorised person\nseizes a thing under this Part, an authorised person must return it if:\n(a) the reason for its seizure no longer exists or it is decided that\nit is not to be used in evidence; or\n(b) the period of 120 days after its seizure ends;\nwhichever first occurs, unless the thing is forfeited or forfeitable to\nthe Commonwealth.\n(2) At the end of the 120 days specified in subsection (1), an\nauthorised person must take reasonable steps to return the thing to\nthe person from whom it was seized, unless:\n(a) proceedings in respect of which the thing may afford\nevidence were instituted before the end of the 120 days and\nhave not been completed (including an appeal to a court in\nrelation to those proceedings); or\n(b) an authorised person may retain the thing because of an order\nunder section 48J; or\n\nAdministration  Chapter 6\nEntry, searches and warrants  Part 6-2\n\nSection 48J\nTherapeutic Goods Act 1989\nCompilation date: 05/09/2025\n(c) an authorised person is otherwise authorised (by a law, or an\norder of a court, of the Commonwealth or of a State or\nTerritory) to retain, destroy or dispose of the thing.\n(3) The thing may be returned under subsection (2) either\nunconditionally or on such terms and conditions as the Secretary\nsees fit.\nNote:\nThis section does not apply in relation to the thing if section 52AAB\napplies in relation to the thing: see subsection 52AAB(2).",
      "skos:definition": "(1) Subject to any contrary order of a court, if an authorised person\nseizes a thing under this Part, an authorised person must return it if:\n(a) the reason for its seizure no longer exists or it is decided that\nit is not to be used in evidence; or\n(b) the period of 120 days after its seizure ends;\nwhichever first occurs, unless the thing is forfeited or forfeitable to\nthe Commonwealth.\n(2) At the end of the 120 days specified in subsection (1), an\nauthorised person must take reasonable steps to return the thing to\nthe person from whom it was seized, unless:\n(a) proceedings in respect of which the thing may afford\nevidence were instituted before the end of the 120 days and\nhave not been completed (including an appeal to a court in\nrelation to those proceedings); or\n(b) an authorised person may retain the thing because of an order\nunder section 48J; or\n\nAdministration  Chapter 6\nEntry, searches and warrants  Part 6-2\n\nSection 48J\nTherapeutic Goods Act 1989\nCompilation date: 05/09/2025\n(c) an authorised person is otherwise authorised (by a law, or an\norder of a court, of the Commonwealth or of a State or\nTerritory) to retain, destroy or dispose of the thing.\n(3) The thing may be returned under subsection (2) either\nunconditionally or on such terms and conditions as the Secretary\nsees fit.\nNote:\nThis section does not apply in relation to the thing if section 52AAB\napplies in relation to the thing: see subsection 52AAB(2).",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-48h-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-48h-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-48h-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "3d65eb0ffaad24f8dd55791c6678ff368c6f882e3849331849b8b91dc782066f"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-48h",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "48H Retention of seized things",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 328,
      "values:originalText": "(1) Subject to any contrary order of a court, if an authorised person\nseizes a thing under this Part, an authorised person must return it if:\n(a) the reason for its seizure no longer exists or it is decided that\nit is not to be used in evidence; or\n(b) the period of 120 days after its seizure ends;\nwhichever first occurs, unless the thing is forfeited or forfeitable to\nthe Commonwealth.\n(2) At the end of the 120 days specified in subsection (1), an\nauthorised person must take reasonable steps to return the thing to\nthe person from whom it was seized, unless:\n(a) proceedings in respect of which the thing may afford\nevidence were instituted before the end of the 120 days and\nhave not been completed (including an appeal to a court in\nrelation to those proceedings); or\n(b) an authorised person may retain the thing because of an order\nunder section 48J; or\n\nAdministration  Chapter 6\nEntry, searches and warrants  Part 6-2\n\nSection 48J\nTherapeutic Goods Act 1989\nCompilation date: 05/09/2025\n(c) an authorised person is otherwise authorised (by a law, or an\norder of a court, of the Commonwealth or of a State or\nTerritory) to retain, destroy or dispose of the thing.\n(3) The thing may be returned under subsection (2) either\nunconditionally or on such terms and conditions as the Secretary\nsees fit.\nNote:\nThis section does not apply in relation to the thing if section 52AAB\napplies in relation to the thing: see subsection 52AAB(2)."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-48h-ss-1",
