    {
      "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-54",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "54 Offences and forfeiture",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-54"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf, p.351",
      "values:originalText": "(3) If a court:\n(a) convicts a person of an offence against this Act; or\n(aa) makes an order under section 19B of the Crimes Act 1914 in\nrespect of a person charged with an offence against this Act;\nor\n(b) orders a person to pay a pecuniary penalty for the\ncontravention of a civil penalty provision;\nin relation to any therapeutic goods or vaping goods, the court may\norder that the goods be forfeited to the Commonwealth and, if an\norder is made, the goods become the property of the\nCommonwealth.\n(4) Where goods are so forfeited, the Secretary may cause notice of the\nforfeiture to be published in the Gazette or on the Department’s\nwebsite.\n\nChapter 7  Miscellaneous\n\nSection 54AA\nTherapeutic Goods Act 1989\nCompilation date: 05/09/2025\n(5) Goods forfeited under an order referred to in subsection (3) are to\nbe disposed of in such manner as the Secretary directs.\nNote:\nSee also Part 6-2A, which provides for forfeiture of things seized in\ncertain circumstances.",
      "skos:definition": "(3) If a court:\n(a) convicts a person of an offence against this Act; or\n(aa) makes an order under section 19B of the Crimes Act 1914 in\nrespect of a person charged with an offence against this Act;\nor\n(b) orders a person to pay a pecuniary penalty for the\ncontravention of a civil penalty provision;\nin relation to any therapeutic goods or vaping goods, the court may\norder that the goods be forfeited to the Commonwealth and, if an\norder is made, the goods become the property of the\nCommonwealth.\n(4) Where goods are so forfeited, the Secretary may cause notice of the\nforfeiture to be published in the Gazette or on the Department’s\nwebsite.\n\nChapter 7  Miscellaneous\n\nSection 54AA\nTherapeutic Goods Act 1989\nCompilation date: 05/09/2025\n(5) Goods forfeited under an order referred to in subsection (3) are to\nbe disposed of in such manner as the Secretary directs.\nNote:\nSee also Part 6-2A, which provides for forfeiture of things seized in\ncertain circumstances.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-54-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-54-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-54-ss-5"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "749e12e28761a66d3c71018e74df320e4fea72751d43f1a6c41b2da013e9f58a"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-54",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "54 Offences and forfeiture",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 351,
      "values:originalText": "(3) If a court:\n(a) convicts a person of an offence against this Act; or\n(aa) makes an order under section 19B of the Crimes Act 1914 in\nrespect of a person charged with an offence against this Act;\nor\n(b) orders a person to pay a pecuniary penalty for the\ncontravention of a civil penalty provision;\nin relation to any therapeutic goods or vaping goods, the court may\norder that the goods be forfeited to the Commonwealth and, if an\norder is made, the goods become the property of the\nCommonwealth.\n(4) Where goods are so forfeited, the Secretary may cause notice of the\nforfeiture to be published in the Gazette or on the Department’s\nwebsite.\n\nChapter 7  Miscellaneous\n\nSection 54AA\nTherapeutic Goods Act 1989\nCompilation date: 05/09/2025\n(5) Goods forfeited under an order referred to in subsection (3) are to\nbe disposed of in such manner as the Secretary directs.\nNote:\nSee also Part 6-2A, which provides for forfeiture of things seized in\ncertain circumstances."
    },
