    {
      "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-54c",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "54C Establishing whether an executive officer took reasonable steps",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-54c"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf, p.358",
      "values:originalText": "to prevent the commission of an offence or the\ncontravention of a civil penalty provision\n(1) For the purposes of section 54B, in determining whether an\nexecutive officer of a body corporate failed to take all reasonable\nsteps to prevent the commission of the offence or the contravention\nof a civil penalty provision, a court is to have regard to:\n(a) what action (if any) the officer took towards ensuring that the\nbody’s employees, agents and contractors have a reasonable\nknowledge and understanding of the requirements to comply\nwith this Act and the regulations, in so far as those\nrequirements affect the employees, agents or contractors\nconcerned; and\n(b) what action (if any) the officer took when he or she became\naware that the body was committing an offence against, or\notherwise contravening, this Act or the regulations.\n(2) This section does not, by implication, limit the generality of\nsection 54B.\n(3) In this section, executive officer has the same meaning as in\nsection 54B.",
      "skos:definition": "to prevent the commission of an offence or the\ncontravention of a civil penalty provision\n(1) For the purposes of section 54B, in determining whether an\nexecutive officer of a body corporate failed to take all reasonable\nsteps to prevent the commission of the offence or the contravention\nof a civil penalty provision, a court is to have regard to:\n(a) what action (if any) the officer took towards ensuring that the\nbody’s employees, agents and contractors have a reasonable\nknowledge and understanding of the requirements to comply\nwith this Act and the regulations, in so far as those\nrequirements affect the employees, agents or contractors\nconcerned; and\n(b) what action (if any) the officer took when he or she became\naware that the body was committing an offence against, or\notherwise contravening, this Act or the regulations.\n(2) This section does not, by implication, limit the generality of\nsection 54B.\n(3) In this section, executive officer has the same meaning as in\nsection 54B.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-54c-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-54c-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-54c-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "53df1947c8d1e25dbc5068e17cfb3734e168c6aa6095c6d250117e5303a33198"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-54c",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "54C Establishing whether an executive officer took reasonable steps",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 358,
      "values:originalText": "to prevent the commission of an offence or the\ncontravention of a civil penalty provision\n(1) For the purposes of section 54B, in determining whether an\nexecutive officer of a body corporate failed to take all reasonable\nsteps to prevent the commission of the offence or the contravention\nof a civil penalty provision, a court is to have regard to:\n(a) what action (if any) the officer took towards ensuring that the\nbody’s employees, agents and contractors have a reasonable\nknowledge and understanding of the requirements to comply\nwith this Act and the regulations, in so far as those\nrequirements affect the employees, agents or contractors\nconcerned; and\n(b) what action (if any) the officer took when he or she became\naware that the body was committing an offence against, or\notherwise contravening, this Act or the regulations.\n(2) This section does not, by implication, limit the generality of\nsection 54B.\n(3) In this section, executive officer has the same meaning as in\nsection 54B."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:therapeutic-goods-act-1989-sec-54c-ss-1",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "54C(1) Establishing whether an executive officer took reasonable steps",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/therapeutic-goods-act-1989#sec-54c-ss-1"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "therapeutic-goods-act-1989.pdf, p.358",
      "values:originalText": "(1) For the purposes of section 54B, in determining whether an\nexecutive officer of a body corporate failed to take all reasonable\nsteps to prevent the commission of the offence or the contravention\nof a civil penalty provision, a court is to have regard to:\n(a) what action (if any) the officer took towards ensuring that the\nbody’s employees, agents and contractors have a reasonable\nknowledge and understanding of the requirements to comply\nwith this Act and the regulations, in so far as those\nrequirements affect the employees, agents or contractors\nconcerned; and\n(b) what action (if any) the officer took when he or she became\naware that the body was committing an offence against, or\notherwise contravening, this Act or the regulations.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) For the purposes of section 54B, in determining whether an\nexecutive officer of a body corporate failed to take all reasonable\nsteps to prevent the commission of the offence or the contravention\nof a civil penalty provision, a court is to have regard to:\n(a) what action (if any) the officer took towards ensuring that the\nbody’s employees, agents and contractors have a reasonable\nknowledge and understanding of the requirements to comply\nwith this Act and the regulations, in so far as those\nrequirements affect the employees, agents or contractors\nconcerned; and\n(b) what action (if any) the officer took when he or she became\naware that the body was committing an offence against, or\notherwise contravening, this Act or the regulations.",
      "values:partOf": {
